{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c001",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The execution date is incomplete on its face — \"the Ist day of ___ 1999\" leaves the month blank, so the date of making cannot be determined from the words. That is a mechanical defect that bears on when obligations attached.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30},
     {"issue": "The preamble dates the making of the Agreement in 1999, while Section 11 (Effective Date) states the Agreement is effective August 1, 1997; the two cannot both be read literally. A court would have to decide which date governs.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c002",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The Custodian's duties are given as a closed enumeration with no catch-all, leaving open whether anything not listed (e.g. monitoring the investment manager or the account's contents) is a duty at all; expressio unius cuts one way and \"be responsible for the collection of all investment income\" cuts the other. The reach of the list is exactly what two parties would fight about.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.42},
     {"issue": "The ministerial, direction-driven duties here sit awkwardly against Section 21's indemnity for loss by \"mysterious disappearance\", which imposes liability untethered to any listed duty. A court could be asked whether Section 1 caps the Custodian's responsibilities.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c003",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "Subsection (c)'s catch-all — \"any other instruments that may be necessary or appropriate to carry out the custodianship duties and powers\" — follows specific documents of transfer and conveyance, so its reach is uncertain; \"ministerial and other non-discretionary duties\" in (b) is likewise a boundary term. The general-vs-specific tension is nameable.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.15}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c004",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"[A] market value for the assets of the account\" fixes neither a valuation source nor a method, but the obligation itself is plain. Only mild vagueness.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.14},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.10}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c005",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"[S]uch other reports as the Owners may reasonably request, including reports ... as may be necessary\" stacks two open-ended standards with no benchmark. The scope of the reporting duty is not fixed by the words.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.14}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c006",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.10},
     {"issue": "Compensation is set \"in Schedule A attached to this Agreement\", but no Schedule A appears in the instrument, and its amendment \"from time to time by the parties\" is not tied to Section 6(a)'s requirement of written agreement. A court would have to supply or reconcile the missing term.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.31}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"[O]ut-of-pocket expenses during the reasonable performance of the Custodian's duties\" misplaces the modifier: it is unclear whether \"reasonable\" limits the expenses or merely describes the performance. That misplacement changes what the Owner must reimburse.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.31},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.09}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The only defect is the typo \"chenges\"/\"conceming\", which does not alter meaning. The obligation to furnish and update the list is definite.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.10},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.10}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"[T]he oral advice as confirmed in writing or written advice of such persons\" is syntactically ambiguous: it may mean the Custodian may act on oral advice only once confirmed in writing, or that written confirmation is merely one of two permitted forms. Whether reliance on unconfirmed oral instructions is protected turns on this reading.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.40},
     {"issue": "Section 1(b)–(c) requires the Custodian to act and settle only on \"written direction\", which is difficult to square with any reading of this clause permitting reliance on oral advice. A court could be asked which controls.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.08},
     {"issue": "Section 4 contemplates Schedule A being \"amended from time to time by the parties\" without any writing requirement, while this clause permits amendment only \"by written agreement of the parties\". The tension is mild but nameable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.19}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"[U]nless or until terminated\" is a clumsy conjunction, but the 30-day written-notice mechanic is plain. Nothing turns on the wording.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.11},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.10}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c012",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.11},
     {"issue": "\"[A]ll securities held in the account shall be delivered\" on termination cannot be squared literally with Section 15, under which deposit securities are under the control of the Colorado Insurance Commissioner and may not be withdrawn without approval, nor with Section 6(d)'s withholding right. A court would have to harmonise them.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c013",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"[B]alances owing to the Custodian\" is undefined and broader than \"fees\", leaving the extent of the withholding right uncertain. The clause also does not say how assets are valued when withheld.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22},
     {"issue": "This self-help right qualifies Section 6(c)'s unqualified command that \"all securities held in the account shall be delivered\" to the Owner. The two sentences pull against each other.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c014",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The carve-out turns on whether the Custodian \"has committed negligence or malfeasance\", terms that are neither defined nor allocated a decision-maker, and the indemnity's reach — whether it covers claims by the Owner itself as well as third parties — is not stated. Both are classic points of construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.40},
     {"issue": "A broad Owner-indemnity for \"any and all claims\" the Custodian incurs by acting as Custodian is circular against Sections 21 and 22, which make the Custodian liable to the Owner for losses including no-fault \"mysterious disappearance\". Read together they can require the Owner to indemnify the Custodian against the very liability Section 21 creates.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.53}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c015",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.05},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.06}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c016",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.05},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.06}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c017",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"[R]elevant to this Agreement\" is the only limit on an otherwise unbounded examination right, with no notice, place or procedure. The boundary of \"relevant\" is left to argument.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.18},
     {"issue": "This unconditional inspection right conflicts with Section 17, which permits examination only during business hours, on the Custodian's premises, on reasonable notice and \"only upon\" written instructions from an appropriate officer of the Owner. Both cannot be applied literally.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.40}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c018",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.06},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.08}
   ]}
]}

